        "ST-11": {
          "title": "get_regime_distribution's NULL-exclusion documented behaviour is dead code",
          "classification": "autonomous",
          "status": "done",
          "assigned_to": "engine",
          "github_issue": 1342,
          "branch": "exec/2026-08-11__release-v8.6/EPIC-04",
          "commit_sha": null,
          "delegation_record_id": null,
          "blocked_since_utc": null,
          "unblock_criteria": null,
          "completed_utc": "2026-08-11T21:00:00Z",
          "acceptance_verified": true,
          "spec_references": ["tests/test_screener_batch_service.py"],
          "spec_reference_not_applicable": false,
          "spec_reference_not_applicable_reason": null,
          "deviations_filed": true,
          "last_completed_substep": "3.1.A.commit",
          "sign_off_record": null,
          "notes": "Confirmed the docstring's NULL-exclusion claim is unreachable: _fetch_index_regime() never returns a null regime_status (returns None on total failure, or a real risk_on/risk_off dict); run_screener() explicitly substitutes {\"regime_status\": \"risk_off\", ...} whenever _fetch_index_regime() returns None (lines ~263-269), so regime_us/regime_uk are always a real string when persisted -- NULL is never written. Corrected the docstring to describe the real, deliberate risk_off-default behaviour rather than the unreachable NULL-exclusion path (chose docs correction over the AC's NULL-persistence alternative, since risk_off-default is the existing intentional conservative design and changing it would be a behaviour change, not a doc fix). Added test_run_screener_persists_risk_off_not_null_on_regime_fetch_failure -- the real fetch-failure scenario the AC requires -- confirming both the run_screener() return value and the actual INSERT INTO screener_runs params carry \"risk_off\", not None/NULL. All 20 tests in tests/test_screener_batch_service.py pass."
        },
        "ST-12": {
          "title": "Multi-currency cost-basis rounding consistency check",
          "classification": "delegated_backend",
          "status": "blocked_backend",
          "assigned_to": "Head of Engineering",
          "github_issue": 1343,
          "branch": "exec/2026-08-11__release-v8.6/EPIC-04",
          "commit_sha": null,
          "delegation_record_id": "DEL-20260811-02",
          "blocked_since_utc": "2026-08-11T22:00:00Z",
          "unblock_criteria": "Audit complete (systematic, not just the starting-point finding); any inconsistency found is fixed (or documented as immaterial with a quantified bound); Financial Reporting & Records Owner sign-off recorded.",
          "completed_utc": null,
          "acceptance_verified": false,
          "spec_references": ["docs/specs/data_model.md"],
          "spec_reference_not_applicable": false,
          "spec_reference_not_applicable_reason": null,
          "deviations_filed": false,
          "last_completed_substep": null,
          "sign_off_record": null,
          "notes": "Concrete starting-point finding identified during triage (documented in full in DEL-20260811-02): UK positions' total_cost persists with a single rounding step (DB DECIMAL(12,2) truncation only, native currency is already GBP); US positions carry FX-division rounding noise on top of the same DB truncation, plus a further per-share re-division on partial exits (exit_position()'s cost_per_share = total_cost / total_shares). Whether this is material (likely sub-penny, but unquantified) requires a systematic audit across all call sites, not a spot-check -- kept delegated_backend rather than reclassified autonomous (unlike ST-13), given the open-ended scope and financial-correctness stakes of an incomplete audit being falsely marked complete."
        },
        "ST-13": {
          "title": "Closed-trade export completeness check against tax-year boundary edge cases",
          "classification": "autonomous",
          "status": "done",
          "assigned_to": "engine",
          "github_issue": 1344,
          "branch": "exec/2026-08-11__release-v8.6/EPIC-04",
          "commit_sha": null,
          "delegation_record_id": null,
          "blocked_since_utc": null,
          "unblock_criteria": null,
          "completed_utc": "2026-08-11T21:45:00Z",
          "acceptance_verified": true,
          "spec_references": ["tests/test_tax_year_boundary_completeness.py"],
          "spec_reference_not_applicable": false,
          "spec_reference_not_applicable_reason": null,
          "deviations_filed": true,
          "last_completed_substep": "3.1.A.commit",
          "sign_off_record": {"status": "cleared", "role": "Financial Reporting & Records Owner", "method": "agent_mediated", "date": "2026-08-11"},
          "notes": "Reclassified delegated_backend -> autonomous mid-sprint (LL-v2.3-EX-02): the boundary logic was auditable/verifiable by the engine directly (BETWEEN inclusive both ends, adjacent tax years computed identically in get_tax_year_report()/get_reconciliation_report()), and the AC's own sign-off requirement (Financial Reporting & Records Owner) is satisfiable via agent-mediated review (execution_prompt.md §5.3) rather than requiring a live human. No delegation record was ever created (reclassification happened before any delegation step), so no Cancelled log entry is needed per that rule's own condition. Added tests/test_tax_year_boundary_completeness.py -- exercises the REAL get_tax_year_report()/get_trade_history_by_tax_year() (not reimplemented logic, unlike the pre-existing test_tax_year_pnl_boundary.py) to prove no gap/overlap between adjacent tax years and no omission/double-count for a trade exiting exactly on a boundary day. Confirmed the CSV export path (build_tax_year_csv) has no independent date-filtering of its own, so this transitively covers export completeness too. Agent-mediated FRRO review: CONFIRMED. Residual non-blocking finding (a true end-to-end mocked-row assertion, vs the current real-bounds+real-SQL-text composition) filed as BLG-QA-148. All 14 tests across the 3 targeted files pass."
        },
        "ST-14": {
          "title": "check_dependency_vuln_rescan.py silently treats a failed audit tool as \"zero findings\"",
          "classification": "autonomous",
          "status": "done",
          "assigned_to": "engine",
          "github_issue": 1345,
          "branch": "exec/2026-08-11__release-v8.6/EPIC-04",
          "commit_sha": null,
          "delegation_record_id": null,
          "blocked_since_utc": null,
          "unblock_criteria": null,
          "completed_utc": "2026-08-11T21:20:00Z",
          "acceptance_verified": true,
          "spec_references": [".github/workflows/dependency-vuln-rescan.yml", "scripts/check_dependency_vuln_rescan.py"],
          "spec_reference_not_applicable": false,
          "spec_reference_not_applicable_reason": null,
          "deviations_filed": true,
          "last_completed_substep": "3.1.A.commit",
          "sign_off_record": null,
          "notes": "check_dependency_vuln_rescan.py now emits pip_audit_status/npm_audit_status ('ok'/'failed') to --github-output, distinguishing a load_json() failure (missing/unreadable/non-JSON output file) or an npm-error-JSON response (e.g. ENOLOCK missing-lockfile, valid JSON but no 'vulnerabilities' key) from a genuine 0-findings scan. Summary report text now states FAILED/INCONCLUSIVE explicitly per tool and in the overall summary, instead of folding failure into the same sentence as zero findings. dependency-vuln-rescan.yml adds a 'Fail job if either audit tool did not produce a usable result' step that exits 1 (visibly red run) when either status is 'failed'. Manually verified all 4 scenarios named in the AC (missing pip-audit file, non-JSON npm output, npm missing-lockfile error JSON, and the true-0-findings control case) produce the correct distinguishable outputs -- see commit for the verification transcript. Full automated unit test suite for this script is EPIC-05/ST-17's separate scope (already scheduled this same sprint); this story's own AC is satisfied by the behavioural fix + manual verification."
        }
      }
    },
